+# Flight 93: two FAA-position transcripts preserve differing phone descriptions
+
+**Finding.** Two separately labeled Commission-release transcripts both record an FAA relay using the terms “911 air phone” and “Cell phone.” They also differ in a short adjacent passage. These are transcripts of different positions with overlapping reported conversation. Their wording does **not** determine the passenger's device, establish that the passages have identical audio sources, or establish why the transcripts differ.
+
+## Sources and exact observations
+
+The [position-14 transcript](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%208%20Files/Team%208%20Box%201/T8%20B1%20ATCSCC%20Tape%20Transcript-%20Position%2014%20Fdr-%20Contents-%20Parts%201%20thru%204.pdf#page=60), PDF pages 60–61, is a 108-page released scan, SHA-256 `94d348e957240ea2cd8260b310ba8f5406a4b1a4dd95a3c79d3b77078e4e42f4`. The [position-15 transcript](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%208%20Files/Team%208%20Box%201/T8%20B1%20ATCSCC%20Tape%20Transcript-%20Position%2015%20Fdr-%20Contents-%20Parts%202%20and%203.pdf#page=81), PDF pages 81–82, is a 161-page released scan, SHA-256 `20ad384d59650e5a08447c600e8ed8b72e6b6391c66120a2984f00688cabd14c`. Both are members of a public mirror of the Commission's January 2009 release, identified by their full ZIP member names in [SOURCE-PINS.json](SOURCE-PINS.json). We acquired them by checked byte ranges and independently verified that the direct member links returned the same full PDF bytes. The specified pages were visually inspected; the quoted words below were also checked with Poppler text extraction.
+
+| Observation | Position 14, pages 60–61 | Position 15, pages 81–82 |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Device terms | Describes an alleged 911 “air phone” report; a separate line reads “Cell phone.” | Also describes an alleged 911 “air phone” report; a separate line reads “Cell phone.” |
+| Nearby overlap | After a notation that two conversations are occurring, the passage says: “In touch with a passenger. Do they still have the passenger on the line?” | Its corresponding area includes “93, cell phone call received from a passenger.” A following line begins “Both were PD.” before asking about contact. |
+
+The position-14 pages do not contain the position-15 sentence quoted above. The two scans nonetheless present substantially overlapping relay language. Their different text could reflect different recordings, overlapping conversations, audibility, transcription choices or other factors; none was tested or selected as the explanation. No conclusion about omission, editing, fabrication or intent follows.
+
+Both transcripts attribute the report to an FAA relay involving Oak Brook police and a passenger reportedly on Flight 93. That is what the transcriptions report, not an independently verified police call, device identification, caller identity, source of the reported details or real-time clock. “Air phone” and “cell phone” are preserved as separate reported words, including the notice of simultaneous conversations; neither is selected as the actual device.
+
+A [previously reviewed 2004 Commission staff note](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/d620577724d8789e6114cec19e5fbf6ad368aa4f/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-ua93-layering-record-445-2026-11-14/README.md) describes a position-4 relay, whereas these two released transcripts are labeled positions 14 and 15. The similar subject matter is a lead for comparison, not proof that any two records contain the same audio or describe the same originating call. The [prior waveform comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/e0cc819ebb6827b69149ac0eb30a073b1fe2ba75/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-ua93-clip-audio-correlation-2026-11-14/README.md) likewise did not pass its prespecified threshold. Do not count these dependent accounts as independent evidence of the passenger's device.
+
+## Verification and remaining tests
+
+- [x] Visually inspected the four cited PDF pages; verified source hashes and the selected text using [VERIFY-v2.py](VERIFY-v2.py). The first checker, [VERIFY-v1.py](VERIFY-v1.py), failed because its target phrase crosses a page boundary and the extractor inserts a heading. The [test history](VERIFY-HISTORY.md) preserves that failure; the corrected checker tests both sides separately. These checks validate selected scan text and source bytes, not speech accuracy or historical events.
+- [ ] Independently listen to the corresponding position-14, position-15 and position-4 recordings; compare audible words, background channels and overlapping speech with each transcript, including unintelligible sections. Establish whether the same transmission is represented.
+- [ ] Obtain the originating 911/dispatch record, operator/carrier logs, source-to-recording chain and independent clock anchors. Distinguish any other Flight 93 calls instead of assuming identity.
+- [ ] Determine whether the transcript differences arise from recording content, audibility, transcription or another cause. The conflicting records remain preserved as printed, with no resolution asserted.
+
+The comparison is restricted to these cited passages; it does not audit the rest of either transcript. Raw scans are preserved in the research worktree and are linked from their public source, not republished here.
